(5926199, 863677, 100393, '<td valign="top">I''m going through the entries, stopping at those images I feel have had the benefit of an unconventional eye and dwelling a little longer to try to see and appreciate what you saw. This is one of those images. <br/> <br/>Positives: Wow - What a pristine and original image, wonderful composition and bold as you like.<br/> <br/>Critical stuff: Nothing at all<br/> <br/>Overall: Quite brilliant really.</td>', 'I''m going through the entries, stopping at those images I feel have had the benefit of an unconventional eye and dwelling a little longer to try to see and appreciate what you saw. This is one of those images.  Positives: Wow - What a pristine and original image, wonderful composition and bold as you like. Critical stuff: Nothing at all Overall: Quite brilliant really.', '2010-04-01 19:11:40.000000', NULL, 1),
(5926211, 864378, 100393, '<td valign="top">I''m going through the entries, stopping at those images I feel have had the benefit of an unconventional eye and dwelling a little longer to try to see and appreciate what you saw. This is one of those images. <br/> <br/>Positives: Easily the most powerful image I''ve seen so far in this FS. So many compelling elements - the figures, than hand, the ambiguity of the scene. And then there is the ''surface'' I think this might be a reflection in metal but it is just as likely to be us looking through perspex. The way you have focussed on the scratches is of course the secret of the image - it keeps us remote from the scene, we feel hidden, nebulous and to an extent disassociated and uninformed.<br/> <br/>Critical stuff: Nothing.<br/> <br/>Overall: 10 and a fav. Top drawer stuff.</td>', 'I''m going through the entries, stopping at those images I feel have had the benefit of an unconventional eye and dwelling a little longer to try to see and appreciate what you saw. This is one of those images.  Positives: Easily the most powerful image I''ve seen so far in this FS. So many compelling elements - the figures, than hand, the ambiguity of the scene. And then there is the ''surface'' I think this might be a reflection in metal but it is just as likely to be us looking through perspex. The way you have focussed on the scratches is of course the secret of the image - it keeps us remote from the scene, we feel hidden, nebulous and to an extent disassociated and uninformed. Critical stuff: Nothing. Overall: 10 and a fav. Top drawer stuff.', '2010-04-01 19:17:34.000000', NULL, 1),
